+# Implementation Plan: S004 Provider retry quota
+
+**Branch**: `S004-provider-retry` | **Date**: 2026-08-10 | **Spec**: [spec.md](spec.md)
+**Input**: Story specification in this directory.
+
+## Summary
+
+Add a per-workspace retry-credit reservation lifecycle around each provider send
+attempt. A transaction atomically creates an idempotently keyed reservation and
+reduces available credit; terminal provider outcomes then finalize or release
+that same reservation. The design preserves the original balance for failed,
+timed-out, or cancelled sends and makes repeated outcome delivery harmless.
+
+## Technical Approach
+
+Use a transactional persistence boundary with a conditional balance update and
+one reservation record per workspace plus attempt id. Starting an attempt
+creates the record in `reserved` state only when the workspace has available
+credit; the transaction otherwise rejects the attempt without changing state.
+Provider outcome handling locks or conditionally updates the reservation by its
+unique attempt id: `accepted` changes `reserved` to `finalized`, while
+`failed`, `timed_out`, and `cancelled` change it to `released` and restore one
+credit in the same transaction. Terminal transitions are compare-and-set so a
+duplicate callback observes the existing terminal state rather than changing a
+balance again. The persistence adapter and provider callback names are left to
+the implementation repository; their behavior is fixed by
+[data-model.md](data-model.md) and
+[retry-credit-reservation.md](contracts/retry-credit-reservation.md).
+
+## Dependencies
+
+- Requires: a workspace-scoped retry-credit balance, a durable send-attempt
+  identifier, a transaction-capable persistence layer, and provider outcomes
+  that distinguish acceptance from failure, timeout, and cancellation.
+- Provides: a stable reservation contract for the reminder send path and any
+  retry policy that consumes workspace credits.
+
+## Testing Strategy
+
+- Unit tests: state-transition and idempotency rules for every reservation
+  outcome, including repeated terminal outcomes.
+- Integration tests: concurrent start attempts against one workspace; assert
+  that accepted sends consume one credit and every non-accepted terminal path
+  restores exactly one.
+- Contract tests: reject invalid transitions and require each callback to carry
+  the durable attempt id and a recognized terminal outcome.
+
+## Technical Context
+
+**Language/Version**: Not established in this planning-only workspace.  
+**Primary Dependencies**: A provider client and transaction-capable persistence
+adapter selected by the implementation repository.  
+**Storage**: Durable transactional store for balances and reservations.  
+**Testing**: The target repository's unit and integration test runner.  
+**Target Platform**: Server-side reminder delivery path.  
+**Project Type**: Service/domain behavior; no UI or source layout exists here.  
+**Performance Goals**: Correctness under concurrent attempts is required;
+latency targets are not specified.  
+**Constraints**: Available balance never becomes negative; a credit is either
+consumed by acceptance or restored once on every other terminal outcome.  
+**Scale/Scope**: One workspace balance and one reservation lifecycle per send
+attempt.
+
+## Architecture Decision Gate
+
+This is a detailed plan because atomic shared-quota mutation and callback
+idempotency are a new cross-cutting persistence pattern. The chosen approach is
+a transaction containing the reservation transition and matching balance update.
+
+| Alternative | Decision | Reason |
+|---|---|---|
+| Read balance, then decrement in application code | Rejected | Concurrent readers can both spend the final credit. |
+| Decrement at attempt start and compensate in a later job | Rejected | A crash or duplicate job can lose or multiply a restore. |
+| Immutable reservation ledger plus derived balance | Deferred | It is viable at higher audit/reporting needs, but the story supplies no such requirement and it adds reconciliation work. |
+| Transactional balance plus idempotent reservation record | Chosen | It makes the invariant local, supports exact-once terminal effects, and is the smallest durable mechanism that handles concurrency. |
+
+## Constitution Check
+
+No project or epic constitution, domain model, UX strategy, design system,
+journey, wireframes, outline, scan report, or research report exists in this
+workspace. The applicable plan gates are therefore derived directly from the
+story specification:
+
+- Correctness: reserve only when an atomic conditional update succeeds; never
+  allow a negative balance.
+- Data integrity: bind a reservation uniquely to the workspace and send-attempt
+  identifier, and transition each reservation at most once to a terminal state.
+- Failure safety: release and restore in the same transaction for provider
+  failure, timeout, and cancellation.
+- UX/brand: no UI is in scope, so `/story-ui-spec` is not required before tasks.
+
+Initial Constitution Check: PASS. The design below uses one stateful pattern
+only where the acceptance criteria require it; no complexity deviation needs
+justification.
+
