+
+## Research Informing This Plan
+
+No external research was needed or performed. The required behavior is fully
+specified by [spec.md](spec.md), and this workspace contains no existing
+research or codebase-scan report to reuse. The transaction and compare-and-set
+approach follows directly from the stated atomicity, non-negative-balance, and
+idempotency requirements.
+
+## Planning Phase 1: Design & Contracts
+
+The planning artifacts are complete:
+
+1. [data-model.md](data-model.md) defines the workspace balance, reservation,
+   and send-attempt relationship plus legal state transitions and invariants.
+2. [retry-credit-reservation.md](contracts/retry-credit-reservation.md) defines
+   the command and callback contract, including duplicate-delivery behavior.
+3. [quickstart.md](quickstart.md) defines the acceptance test scenarios that
+   the implementation and its integration tests must demonstrate.
+
+### Symmetric credit handling
+
+The same story owns both halves of the quota operation. A successful provider
+acceptance finalizes the reservation and leaves the credit consumed. Provider
+failure, timeout, cancellation, and rollback release the reservation and add
+one credit back atomically. No later story is needed to repair a failed attempt.
+
+## Planning Phase 2: Implementation Guidance (For /story-tasks)
+
+### Functional Requirements Extraction
+
+| FR ID | Requirement Summary | Acceptance Criteria | Priority |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| FR-001 | Atomically reserve one workspace credit when an attempt begins. | Reservation succeeds only with available credit; concurrent attempts never leave a negative balance. | Critical |
+| FR-002 | Finalize an accepted provider send once. | Accepted outcome changes a reserved record to finalized; duplicates do nothing. | Critical |
+| FR-003 | Restore credit for non-accepted terminal outcomes once. | Failure, timeout, and cancellation each release the reservation and restore one credit; duplicates do nothing. | Critical |
+| FR-004 | Correlate lifecycle events by attempt. | Each reservation has a unique workspace-plus-attempt identity and rejects conflicting callbacks. | High |
+
+### Implementation sequence for task generation
+
+1. Add or adapt the transactional balance and reservation persistence model.
+2. Add the atomic reserve operation before any provider dispatch.
+3. Add accepted and non-accepted terminal transition handlers using the same
+   attempt id.
+4. Write unit, contract, and concurrent integration tests from
+   [quickstart.md](quickstart.md) before considering the work complete.
+
+Tasks should reuse existing persistence and provider abstractions where the
+target repository has them; they must not introduce a new framework or external
+dependency without a separate decision.
+
+### Security and reliability checklist for task generation
+
+- Validate workspace scope and attempt id at every command and callback.
+- Treat an unknown, conflicting, or already-terminal reservation as a no-op or
+  explicit domain result; never mutate the balance a second time.
+- Run transition and balance changes in one transaction.
+- Test rollback when a transaction fails between reservation state change and
+  balance mutation.
+- Ensure cancellation follows the same release path as provider failure.
+
+## Planning Phase 3: Task Planning Approach
+
+`/story-tasks` should derive ordered tasks from the four functional requirements
+and Phase 1 artifacts. It should put model/transaction work before provider-path
+integration, then add unit and integration tests that exercise all terminal
+states and parallel reservation attempts. No `tasks.md` is created by this
+planning phase.
